<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

	<!--
		Ark.oAuth.Oidc — the OAuth 2.1 / OpenID Connect provider, packaged for nuget.org.

		Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web" with OutputType=Library is deliberate. The output is a class
		library, but it ships MVC controllers and Razor views, so it needs the Web SDK's Razor
		compilation and its implicit FrameworkReference to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App. The price is
		that the Web SDK also treats appsettings/wwwroot as *application* content and would pack it
		into every consuming project — see the "Package payload" group below, which turns that off.
	-->

	<!-- ============================== Compilation ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
		<OutputType>Library</OutputType>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
		<NeutralLanguage>en</NeutralLanguage>
		<!--
			Pinned rather than left to default from the file name: MigrationScript builds resource
			names by hand ("Ark.oAuth.Oidc.Migration.{provider}.up.00001_sql.sql"), so a rename of
			the project file would silently break every migration lookup at runtime.
		-->
		<RootNamespace>Ark.oAuth.Oidc</RootNamespace>
		<AssemblyName>Ark.oAuth.Oidc</AssemblyName>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ============================== Version ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<Version>2.1.1</Version>
		<AssemblyVersion>2.1.1.0</AssemblyVersion>
		<FileVersion>2.1.1.0</FileVersion>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ============================== Package identity ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>true</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<PackageId>Ark.oAuth.Oidc</PackageId>
		<Title>Ark Identity Server — OAuth 2.1 / OpenID Connect provider</Title>
		<Description>A self-contained OAuth 2.1 and OpenID Connect provider for ASP.NET Core. Two lines in Program.cs give you authorization code + PKCE, refresh tokens, client credentials, the device grant, PAR, introspection, revocation, dynamic client registration, RP-initiated logout and a discovery document any standard OIDC library can configure itself from. Multi-tenant, runs on SQLite, MySQL or PostgreSQL, and ships its own sign-in, consent and admin UI. Pair it with Ark.oAuth.Client for the application side.</Description>
		<Authors>Immanuel R</Authors>
		<Company>Immanuel</Company>
		<Product>Ark Identity Server</Product>
		<Copyright>Copyright © 2022-2026 Immanuel</Copyright>
		<PackageTags>oauth oauth2 oauth2.1 oidc openid openid-connect identity identity-provider idp identity-server authentication authorization jwt jwks pkce sso single-sign-on aspnetcore security</PackageTags>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/ir-dev/ark-oauth-oidc</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>false</PackageRequireLicenseAcceptance>
		<PackageIcon>ark_logo.png</PackageIcon>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<DevelopmentDependency>false</DevelopmentDependency>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>
Next release — a landing page after sign-in. Additive.

* **The wizard asks where sign-in lands.** The Server step has a landing page, prefilled with the
  public URL (which opens the console). A page of this app is stored as a path in
  ark_oauth_server:Admin:LandingUrl, so it survives a change of public URL; an address elsewhere is
  stored as given. Sign-in's own routes are refused, since landing there would loop.
* **The page you were on wins.** The choice is made once the tokens are validated: a sign-in
  started on the way to a page (carried through the IdP in state, as before) goes back to it; one
  started at / lands on the landing page.
* **/ark-idp/signin?returnUrl=/a/page** signs in and goes there, for a host page's "Sign in" link.
  A returnUrl that is not a local path is ignored. The auth-error page's "Try again" uses it.
* Unchanged for existing servers: no LandingUrl means / ends in the console, as it did.

Earlier — start with nothing configured: the setup wizard. Additive; suggest 2.2.0.

* **Two lines, no appsettings.** builder.AddArkIdp() and app.UseArkIdp(). Started with no TenantId or
  no connection string, every browser request goes to /ark-idp/setup (other requests get 503
  setup_required), and the wizard collects the organization, logos, public URL, tenant, database
  (SQLite / PostgreSQL / MySQL, with a connection test), the RS256 signing key (generated, 2048 to
  4096 bits, or imported from PEM), the administrator, users and SMTP (with a test message),
  protocol options, CORS origins and a machine client secret.
* **The same process becomes the IdP.** Install writes ark-idp-data/settings.json, creates and seeds
  the database with what was entered, and reloads configuration. The console's sign-in options and
  the CORS policy follow the reload, so / leads straight to the sign-in page. No restart.
* **Secrets stay out of the file.** The administrator password and machine secret are stored only as
  hashes, the signing key only in signing_keys. settings.json is 0600, its directory ignores itself
  in git, and it sits below user secrets, environment and command line, so any value can still be
  overridden. Logos are stored beside it and served from /ark-idp/branding, so emails can show them.
* **Not a race to the first visitor.** From the server's own machine the wizard is open; from
  anywhere else it needs the setup code printed in the start-up log. Setup:RequireToken asks for it
  everywhere; Setup:Enabled=false removes the wizard; Setup:DataPath moves the directory (containers).
* **The keys, once, in a file.** The last screen downloads the issuer, the signing key pair (kid,
  public and private PEM) and the machine client id and secret as JSON or Excel (.xlsx), built in
  the browser; the server never returns the private key or the secret again.
* **Built-in console sign-in.** AddArkIdp registers a cookie plus the framework's OIDC handler against
  this same server, with discovery and keys read in-process rather than fetched from its own public
  address. Hosts that bring Ark.oAuth.Client keep doing so (AddArkIdp(o => o.ConsoleSignIn = false),
  or the existing calls).
* **Existing hosts are unchanged.** A server configured through appsettings or the environment never
  sees the wizard. UseArkAuthData now puts the wizard in front of the bootstrap, so hosts built on
  the lower-level calls get it too when they start unconfigured. An existing Ark database can be
  attached through the wizard instead of seeded.
* New dependency: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect 9.0.x (IdentityModel stays on
  8.8.0).

Earlier — the management API answers to the operator only. BREAKING: ship as 2.1.0.

* **The package decides who may call /api/oauth/v1/….** It used to be a bare [Authorize] against the
  host's default scheme: with the console cookie as the default, any account mapped to the console
  client could create clients and reset secrets in every tenant; with a bearer scheme as the default,
  any token this server issued could. The ArkOperator policy admits the operator's console session
  (AdminUser:Username, or an admin claim on the console mapping) and bearer tokens issued to
  {TenantId}_machine with client.register — validated with this server's own keys, no host wiring.
  Everyone else gets 401/403 JSON, never a sign-in redirect. Upgrade: give other console users the
  admin claim, and delete any bearer scheme a host added for this API.
* **The machine client's secret from configuration.** ark_oauth_server:Management:MachineClientSecret
  is applied on start-up and rotated by changing it, so a pipeline-built deployment can provision on
  first boot. At least 16 characters. While set, the console refuses to regenerate that secret.
* **client.register cannot be self-granted.** Provisioning and dynamic registration refuse it; only an
  operator grants it in the console.
* **/api/migration is off by default** (Management:AllowMigrationEndpoint) and answers 404 while off.
* Optional Management:OperatorDeviceClients admits operator-approved device-code tokens, for a CLI.

Also in the next release — export, import and factory reset, on an operator-only Data page.

* **Export everything.** One JSON file with every mapped table and column — tenants, signing keys,
  clients (with secret hashes), users (with password hashes), access mappings, scopes, claims,
  sessions, refresh tokens, consents, audit trail — plus the app settings, secrets redacted unless
  asked for. Read from the EF model, so a column a later release adds is exported without a change
  here. Optionally encrypted with a passphrase (AES-256-GCM, PBKDF2-SHA256), always checksummed.
* **Import that says what it will do before it does it.** Analyse works out all three modes —
  replace, merge keeping existing records, merge taking the file's — against the database as it is,
  row by row. Rows that cannot be loaded safely (case-only collisions, broken references, duplicate
  names, unsafe redirect URIs, conflicting key material, expired grants…) are excluded by a named
  rule along with everything that depends on them; the rest loads. Apply runs the chosen plan in one
  transaction and refuses if the plan changed since it was shown.
* **Factory reset.** Empties every table and runs the same seed a new database gets — after first
  checking the seed can run, so a reset can never leave a server nobody can sign in to.
* **Additive.** No schema change, no migration, no change to an existing route. Switches under
  ark_oauth_server:Admin:DataTools (Enabled, AllowReset, AllowImport, AllowReplace,
  MaxImportMegabytes), all on by default. Specified in API-GUIDE.md.

Also in the next release — themes and text size chosen by the viewer, and a "Powered by" mark.

* **Appearance picker on every page the package draws.** Sign-in, consent, device, signed-out,
  error, password reset, the admin console and the setup wizard all carry an Appearance button:
  System, Light, Dark, Midnight, Graphite, Forest, Rose, Paper, High contrast, High contrast dark,
  or Custom (light/dark mode, accent and background, with borders, muted text and button labels
  derived to stay readable). Text size from 80% to 160%, every font-size following one variable.
* **Saved per browser, applied before first paint.** localStorage on the IdP's origin, so a choice
  made at sign-in holds in the console, and other open tabs follow. Under the wizard's strict CSP
  too: values are set through CSSOM, never inline styles. Without storage, or without the script,
  the pages render exactly as before.
* **Powered by Ark Identity Server.** The Ark shield under the sign-in card, in the console footer
  and as the wizard's mark. New anonymous assets at /ark-admin/asset/: ark-theme.js, ark-theme.css,
  ark-shield-64.png, ark-shield-512.png. Optional: ark_oauth_server:ShowPoweredBy=false hides it on
  the sign-in pages and in the console footer (the version stays); the setup wizard asks.
* The password-reset confirmation page (/oauth/PwdResetThank) is rebuilt on the shared layout; it was
  the last page of the reset flow still on its own stylesheet.

2.0.3 —back-channel logout, sign-out that covers the whole browser, and a dependency graph that
is only what this package uses.

* **Back-Channel Logout 1.0.** A client that registers a backchannel_logout_uri is now POSTed a
  signed logout token when a session it took part in ends — an RP-initiated logout, a revoked
  refresh token, or an account deactivated mid-session. The token is the §2.4 artefact: typ
  "logout+jwt", the backchannel-logout event claim, sub and sid, and deliberately no nonce, so a
  client cannot be handed one where an ID token was expected.
* **A client that is down cannot stop a user signing out.** The sessions are revoked at this
  server before the first notification goes out, every delivery runs in parallel behind its own
  timeout, and a refusal is written to the audit trail as a warning rather than raised. The worst
  outcome of an unreachable client is an application cookie that outlives the IdP session — which
  is exactly the state every deployment was in before this existed.
* **Clients are notified because they took part, not because they still hold a token.** A
  session_clients row is written the first time a session issues a client an authorization code or
  approves its device request. Deriving that list from live refresh tokens instead would miss
  every client that never asked for offline_access — most of them — and every client whose tokens
  have expired while its own application cookie is still valid.
* **Registering for it.** backchannel_logout_uri and backchannel_logout_session_required are
  accepted through dynamic client registration (§3.1 metadata: absolute, no fragment, https unless
  loopback), on POST /api/oauth/v1/provision/client, and in the admin console's client editor. A
  client with no URI is simply never notified, so nothing changes for clients that do not ask.
* **Signing out now ends every session in the browser, not just the newest.** The session cookie
  holds one sid and each sign-in overwrites it, so on a shared machine the previous person's
  session stayed live in the database — with its refresh tokens, and with them still signed in at
  every application they had opened — while /oauth2/logout ended only the session the cookie
  happened to name. A long-lived ark_idp_bid cookie now identifies the user agent, sessions record
  which browser they were created in, and logout ends all of them. It carries no identity of its
  own: it is a random value that means nothing without the session rows pointing at it.
* **Discovery now tells the truth about logout.** backchannel_logout_supported and
  backchannel_logout_session_supported are advertised. frontchannel_logout_supported and
  frontchannel_logout_session_supported, which were advertised as true against an endpoint this
  server has never had, are now false — a client that reads the document no longer configures a
  logout flow that cannot work.
* **The signed-out page says what actually happened**: how many sessions and accounts were ended
  when it was more than one, how many applications were signed out with you, and — named rather
  than hidden — how many could not be reached and may still be open.
* New settings under ark_oauth_server:Oidc, all defaulted to the behaviour above:
  EnableBackChannelLogout (true), BackChannelLogoutTimeoutSeconds (5), LogoutTokenLifetimeSeconds
  (120), SignOutAllBrowserSessions (true) and SignOutAcrossTenants (true). The last is worth a
  look if one browser is expected to hold sessions in several tenants at once: the browser cookie
  is one per deployment, not one per tenant.
* **Dependencies are now what this package actually uses.** Ark.EfCore, Azure.Identity and
  BouncyCastle are gone. Nothing here ever used a type from Ark.EfCore — it was acting purely as a
  carrier for the EF packages it happened to depend on, which meant the EF version was decided by
  somebody else's release, was pinned at 8.0.x on a net9.0 target, and dragged in a SQL Server
  provider stack (Microsoft.Data.SqlClient and its native SNI runtime) that nothing in this
  package can select. EF Core 9.0.x and the Sqlite, MySQL and PostgreSQL providers are now named
  directly. SQL Server is deliberately absent: Provider "sqlserver" fell through every branch of
  OnConfiguring and left the context with no provider configured, so it has never been a working
  option and shipping the client library implied otherwise.

Migration: existing databases need 00005, which adds clients.backchannel_logout_uri and
clients.backchannel_logout_session_required, sessions.browser_id, and the session_clients table.
Every change is additive and defaulted — existing clients register no logout URI and are not
notified, and sessions that predate the column have no browser to name and are treated as their
own. On SQLite it is applied on start-up and recorded in ark_schema_history, so there is nothing
to run by hand. Only the SQLite scripts ship in this package: on MySQL or PostgreSQL the
equivalent ALTER TABLE and CREATE TABLE are still yours to apply, and skipping them fails the same
way a missing 00004 did — the entities carry columns the tables do not.

2.0.2 — provisioning, activation, branding, and self-applying schema updates.

* **Provisioning API.** POST /api/oauth/v1/provision/client registers an application, creates or
  reuses a user, and adds the user-client access mapping in one call. An existing client name is
  refused with HTTP 409 and code "client_exists" and nothing is written — a live application's
  redirect URIs are never silently rewritten. An existing user is reused and mapped, which is what
  happens when somebody is given their second application. A user this creates signs in
  immediately on ark_oauth_server:DefaultPw; pass send_activation_email to email a link instead.
* **Activation, at two levels.** Applications already had is_active; accounts now have one too.
  POST /api/oauth/v1/activation/client and /activation/user switch either off, and revoke what has
  already been handed out — a deactivated client loses its refresh tokens, a deactivated user
  loses their sessions and refresh tokens — so the switch takes effect now rather than whenever
  they happened to expire. The switch is honoured through the plain client/user upsert too.
* **Sign-in says which level is off.** A deactivated application names itself on the authorization
  endpoint; a deactivated account gets "Your account has been deactivated…" on the sign-in page.
  Both only after the password has been verified, so the single deliberately-vague message for
  every credentials failure — and with it the page's resistance to account enumeration — is
  unchanged.
* **Pending schema scripts run on start-up.** The scripts in Migration/{provider}/up were an
  operator's job after every upgrade, and skipping one failed quietly and specifically: without
  00004 the entity carried users.is_active and the table did not, so /api/oauth/v1/user/list
  answered a bare 500 and the console's Users grid came up empty with nothing saying why. The
  bootstrap now applies what is pending and records it in ark_schema_history.
* **Nothing is replayed.** A database that predates the history table is measured rather than
  replayed: each script is checked against the live schema and one whose tables and columns are all
  present is recorded as a baseline without executing. 00003 rewrites every client that holds no
  secret — right for a 2.0.0 upgrade, wrong for anything after it — so that distinction is the
  whole point. A database created by EnsureCreated on this version records all four and runs none.
* **Provisioning and Activation have their own console page**, /{tenant}/admin/provisioning, linked
  from a navigation bar in the console's top bar and restricted to the administrator account
  (AdminUser:Username, or a principal carrying an "admin" claim). It carries the provisioning form,
  both activation switches, and the state of every client and account at once.
* **The page writes the curl for you.** Fill either form in and the equivalent command appears,
  carrying the values that are in the forms — the provisioning body, the activation body, and a
  client_credentials token step. The secret is the one thing the page cannot read back, so a button
  mints a new one for the machine client and drops it straight into the command.
* **Client logos.** Uploaded in the client editor or at provisioning time and inlined as a data
  URI, so nothing has to be hosted. The sign-in page header now carries the host logo and the
  client logo together, and lays itself out for two marks, one, or none — the previous header
  showed only the client's, with the host's relegated to the footer. The console's top bar shows
  the same lockup, and the client grid shows each client's mark.
* **The client setup page can run the flow.** /{tenant}/oauth2/integrate/{client_id} gained a live
  authorization code + PKCE section: it generates a real verifier and S256 challenge in the
  browser, runs the request in an embedded iframe ending at the redirect_uri you pick, and shows
  the token-exchange command for the code that comes back.
* The interactive pages now send Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self' — clickjacking
  protection they did not have, and what lets the setup page frame the authorization endpoint.
* The management API's list endpoints report a failure as { error, msg } like every other endpoint
  instead of an empty 500, and /api/migration/v1/sql now reports whether the script it ran actually
  worked rather than always answering "executed".

Migration: existing databases need 00004, which adds users.is_active. It is one additive, defaulted
column, so every existing account stays active. This version applies it on start-up, so there is
nothing to run by hand, and a database created by this version already has it.

2.0.1 — maintenance release.

No API or protocol change over 2.0.0. Rebuilt and republished so the package on nuget.org matches
the 2.0.0 source tree exactly, and to carry the packaging fixes below. Consumers on 2.0.0 can
upgrade with no code change; the 2.0.0 notes apply unchanged and are kept below.

Packaging: the package icon (ark_logo.png) and the readme are packed at the root of the .nupkg, so
nuget.org and the Visual Studio package manager show both. The icon is referenced through
PackageIcon rather than the deprecated PackageIconUrl, and is included with an empty PackagePath so
it lands at the package root on a non-Windows build too.

2.0.0 — the standards release.

* Full OAuth 2.1 / OpenID Connect protocol core: discovery, JWKS with two-phase key rotation,
  authorization code + PKCE (S256 only, required for public clients), refresh token rotation,
  client credentials, device authorization grant (RFC 8628), pushed authorization requests
  (RFC 9126), introspection (RFC 7662), revocation (RFC 7009), dynamic client registration
  (RFC 7591/7592), RP-initiated logout, and issuer identification (RFC 9207).
* Access tokens now follow RFC 9068 (typ: at+jwt, client_id, jti, scope).
* Codes and refresh tokens are stored as SHA-256 hashes; replay of either is treated as compromise
  and revokes the derived tokens or the whole refresh-token family.
* Per-client setup page at /{tenant}/oauth2/integrate/{client_id}, generated from the client's own
  registration, with copy-paste config for the Ark client, the raw ASP.NET Core handler,
  oidc-client-ts, Authlib and go-oidc.
* Sign-in and consent pages load no third-party resources and work under a strict CSP.
* The admin console now ships inside this package, at /{tenant}/admin — tenants, clients, users,
  scopes, claims and the access mapping, with no view, stylesheet or script to copy into the host.
  Its CSS and JS are served from the assembly at /ark-admin/asset/{file}. Point
  ark_oauth_server:Admin:SignOutUrl at your own sign-out route, since only the host can drop its
  own authentication cookie. The v1 console at /oauth/{tenant}/v1/server/{client}/manage is still
  served and no longer developed.

Breaking / migration: the administrator account seeded into a new database now comes from
ark_oauth_server:AdminUser (Username, Password, Name) instead of the compiled-in "admin" / "admin".
Password is required, falls back to DefaultPw, and a value left as a &lt;&lt;placeholder&gt;&gt;
counts as unset — seeding stops with a message naming the setting rather than creating an account
whose credentials are published in the release notes of every deployment. Existing databases are
untouched: the section is only read while the schema is being created.

The implicit grant, the hybrid flow and the resource owner password
credentials grant are not supported. The v1 /oauth/{tenant}/v1/... routes still work and keep their
request and response shapes, but now delegate to the protocol core — so codes issued there are
single-use, expire, and have their PKCE verifier checked. Existing databases must run migration
00003 (GET /api/migration/v1/sql?action=up&amp;name=00003_sql.sql), which adds the protocol state
tables and RFC 7591 metadata columns, seeds the scope catalogue, and adopts each tenant's existing
RSA key as its active signing key with kid = tenant_id, so tokens already issued keep validating.

Packaging: the package no longer ships application content or static web assets into consuming
projects, and now carries a readme, an SPDX license expression and a symbol package.
		</PackageReleaseNotes>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ============================== Repository / Source Link ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/ir-dev/ark-oauth-oidc</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<RepositoryBranch>main</RepositoryBranch>
		<PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
		<EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
		<IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
		<SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
		<DebugType>portable</DebugType>
		<!-- Deterministic paths only on a CI build; locally they make stack traces unreadable. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true' or '$(TF_BUILD)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ============================== API documentation ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- Ship IntelliSense for what is documented without demanding a doc comment on every
		     public member before the package can build. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ============================== Package payload ============================== -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<!--
			Without this the Web SDK emits a staticwebassets/ folder plus two build/*.props files
			that wire wwwroot up to be served from _content/Ark.oAuth.Oidc/. Nothing here is served
			that way — see the wwwroot group below — so it was dead weight in the package.

			Note this does NOT stop wwwroot being packed: with static web asset processing off, any
			surviving Content item would be packed as plain content/ instead. The Content Remove
			below is what actually keeps it out.
		-->
		<StaticWebAssetsEnabled>false</StaticWebAssetsEnabled>
		<!--
			IncludeContentInPack is deliberately NOT set to false here. It reads like the switch for
			"do not ship application content", but in NuGet.Build.Tasks.Pack.targets it gates the
			whole _GetPackageFiles target — which is also what collects None items marked
			Pack="true". Setting it false silently drops the icon and the readme and the build fails
			with NU5046. Unwanted content is excluded item by item instead.
		-->
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- ==============================================================================
		Package root files. PackagePath is empty, not "\" — on a non-Windows build MSBuild takes
		the backslash literally and the icon lands in a directory called "\", which pack then
		reports as NU5046 "the icon file does not exist in the package".
	     ============================================================================== -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Icon\ark_logo.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="" Visible="false" />
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- ==============================================================================
		AI-prompt-first developer documentation, packed at the .nupkg root beside the
		README so it travels with the package. nuget.org renders only PackageReadmeFile
		(README.md), so README links to these; they are here for a developer or an AI
		coding agent that has the package in hand (unpack, or browse the repo).

		Same Pack="true" PackagePath="" pattern as README: it lands in the package but is
		NOT contentFiles, so it is never copied into a consuming project's tree. PackagePath
		is empty, not "\", for the non-Windows build reason documented above.
	     ============================================================================== -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="AI-PROMPT.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="AI-RECIPES.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="API-GUIDE.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="TROUBLESHOOTING.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="MIGRATION.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- ==============================================================================
		wwwroot: embedded, never served.

		AddArkOidcServer runs EmbeddedResourceUnpacker in Development, which writes every embedded
		resource out to the host's content root. That is how a host which only references this
		package ends up with the sign-in CSS, the activation email template and the images on disk.
		Removing the same files from Content stops the Web SDK claiming them as application content.
	     ============================================================================== -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<Content Remove="wwwroot\**" />
		<EmbeddedResource Include="wwwroot\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- ==============================================================================
		Migration scripts, looked up by name at runtime as
		"Ark.oAuth.Oidc.Migration.{Sqlite|Mysql|Postgres|Sqlserver}.{up|down}.{file}.sql".
		Wildcarded so a new script is picked up by adding the file and nothing else.
	     ============================================================================== -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<None Remove="Migration\**\*.sql" />
		<EmbeddedResource Include="Migration\**\*.sql" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- ==============================================================================
		Dependencies.

		Every EF Core package below used to arrive transitively, through a reference to
		Ark.EfCore 0.0.1.3. Nothing in this project ever used a type from that package — its
		public surface is one DbContext subclass, one settings POCO and one AddArkContext<T>()
		extension, and this project has its own ArkDataContext and its own registration — so it
		was acting purely as a carrier for the four EF provider packages it happened to depend on.
		That is worth avoiding: the EF version this package built against was decided by somebody
		else's release, it pinned EF at 8.0.x on a net9.0 target, and it dragged in a SQL Server
		provider stack (Microsoft.Data.SqlClient plus its native SNI runtime) that
		ArkDataContext.OnConfiguring has no branch to select. The providers are now named here.

		Sqlite / MySQL / Postgres are the three OnConfiguring can actually switch on, so they are
		the three that are referenced. SQL Server is deliberately absent: setting Provider to
		"sqlserver" fell through every branch and left the context with no provider configured, so
		it has never been a working option and shipping the client library implied otherwise.
	     ============================================================================== -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="ark.net.util" Version="1.0.4.15" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.19" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="9.0.19" />
		<PackageReference Include="MySql.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.17" />
		<PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="9.0.4" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" Version="8.8.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt" Version="8.8.0" />
		<!--
			Not used by this assembly — pinned to keep the graph on one Microsoft.IdentityModel
			version. MySql.Data (under MySql.EntityFrameworkCore) asks for
			Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 7.5.0 while everything else here is on
			8.8.0, and that split does not fail at restore or at build: it fails at the first OIDC
			challenge, with a MethodAccessException or a missing-method error that names neither
			package. Raising the floor to 8.8.0 keeps Protocols and Tokens on the same major.
		-->
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect" Version="8.8.0" />
		<!--
			The built-in console sign-in that AddArkIdp registers: the framework's own OIDC handler,
			pointed at this server. Same patch train as the EF packages above; it asks for
			Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 8.0.x, which the pin above lifts to 8.8.0.
		-->
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect" Version="9.0.10" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
